    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm studying for sec+ and i'm kinda stuck on one thing. if the password is already being hashed, why do we even need to salt it? wouldnt the hash already make it secure anyway?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Salting a hashed password adds an extra layer of security. Even if two users have the same password, the salt value ensures that their hashed passwords will be different. This makes it much harder for attackers to use precomputed hashes (rainbow tables) to crack the passwords. Essentially, salting enhances the security of already hashed passwords by providing uniqueness and defending against brute force and rainbow table attacks.",
