    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it's basically about making the workload too big for them to actually finish. i guess i'm just trying to visualize it. is there a good way to think about the difference between just hashing a password vs hashing it with a salt? like a real-world analogy that isn't just \"it's like adding spice to food\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine an artist (the password) creating signature paintings (the hashes) for an art gallery (the system). Without a signature, a copycat could potentially republish these without anyone noticing (akin to using a hash alone without salt).\n\nNow, think of the signature as your key signature, unique and hard to forge—they often wear the same coat or carry a quill in a distinctive way (the salt). Each painting they create carries this signature. Even if they create th secured same that the gallery (an attacker with a rainbow table) could see multiple paintings, each one carries a unique, invisible signature that can't be copied for duplication, but needs to be matched one by one with its artwork, making forgery time-consuming and nearly impossible.\n\nThis demonstrates how adding a salt increases security—it's personalized for each password, making it extremely challenging to hack trying to reverse-engineer or replicate.",
